// ═══ WHERE THE BLOWER CAN LIVE WHEN THE OPERATOR ALLOWS 70 mm BEHIND THE PLUG END ═══
//
// The operator's chassis (product.printed_chassis.chassis_clearances) ALLOWS 70 mm from a card's plug end
// to the rear chassis fan -- their word, authored as a conservative bound under a rough ~73 mm reading,
// not a caliper reading (DeclaredConservativeByOperator, and gunbc.design_ruling
// a16_rear_clearance_and_sides_are_operator_declared_bounds for the ruling and the quotes). The existing straight-back duct does not fit in it: with the outlet's
// edge on Delta's drawing still unread (extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_outlet_standing), the socket
// bounds the housing at its LONGER edge behind the end -- product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct
// blower_reach_behind = 97.2 mm -- plus the socket's 10 mm depth and a wall, so the straight-back home
// asks the chassis for about 108 mm where it has 70. The duct has to curve.
//
// WHAT THE CURVE ACTUALLY BUYS, AND WHAT IT COSTS. A radial blower is a box whose intake axis is its
// thickness axis and whose outlet lies on the periphery. Two orientations are available and each spends
// a DIFFERENT chassis gap -- this is the whole design, and the model says so in the open:
//
//   PLANE-ON-END      the fan's face lies parallel to the card's end face, its intake looking straight
//                     back. The extent along the card's length axis is the housing's THICKNESS -- 34 mm
//                     with tolerance -- so the 70 mm is satisfied with room to spare. But the fan's face
//                     (97.2 x 94.4 mm) now spans the slot direction, where the card is only 38.6 mm and
//                     the neighbouring card's duct starts at the slot pitch, 40.64 mm: the face overhangs
//                     its own lane by 28.28 mm on EACH side. The outlet points sideways or up, so a leg
//                     has to turn the air forward into the window -- that turn is the curve.
//
// WHAT CHANGED 2026-10-26, AND WHAT DID NOT. The operator ruled on both of this home's blanks: 70 mm
// behind the end, and "more than enough space on the sides". That converts the home from refused-by-an-
// unmeasured-gap to ADMITTED ON AN OPERATOR BOUND -- a verdict of its own, not RouteAdmitted, because the
// bound is a ruling about what the design may spend and not a report of what the chassis is. Their ruling
// also carried the print: the fit print is the instrument a wrong allowance is caught by, and it costs one
// PLA plate. What did NOT change is the arithmetic of the other homes (deck-to-lid and the free side lane
// are still unread and still refuse), the outlet's unread position on Delta's housing, and the fact that
// nobody has measured anything here.
//
//   PLANE-ACROSS-ROW  the fan stands on its side wall, its outlet facing the window directly, as the
//                     existing duct does: only the 33 mm thickness crosses the slot, which the 40.64 mm
//                     pitch tolerates. But then its 97.2 mm face is along the card's length axis and the
//                     70 mm refuses -- unless the fan is lifted out of the lane: OVER THE ROW, or BESIDE
//                     THE ROW, where its long face is no longer fighting the pitch.
//
// So the 70 mm is NOT the only constraint, and saying it is would send the operator to measure the wrong
// thing. Every home that satisfies the 70 mm puts a 94-97 mm housing face somewhere the chassis has never
// been measured, and every home that is not admitted says which reading it still needs. THE REFUSALS ARE
// STILL THE DELIVERABLE for the homes nobody has ruled on; the one home the operator has ruled on is
// admitted, on their bound, with the print still held.
//
// Frame (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct): x along the end face from its TOP end, y across the face from
// its BACK face (the deck side), z along the card's length with the plug end toward the rear fan.

type BlowerHome
  = PlaneOnEnd                       // 34 mm along the card axis; 94-97 mm across the slot
  | PlaneAcrossRowStanding           // the existing straight-back home
  | OverTheRow { riser: Micrometer } // fan above the card row on a riser, outlet leg turned down behind the end
  | BesideTheRow                     // fan in a free lane beside the outermost card

fn blower_home_display(h: BlowerHome) -> String {
  match h {
    PlaneOnEnd => "fan plane on the end (intake looking back, outlet turned by a leg)"
    PlaneAcrossRowStanding => "fan on its side wall behind the plug end (straight back, as built today)"
    OverTheRow {riser: r} => join(["fan over the card row on a ", to_string(value: um(m: r) / 1000), " mm riser, outlet leg turned down behind the end"], "")
    BesideTheRow => "fan beside the row in a free lane"
  }
}

// ═══ EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS DERIVED, NONE IS TYPED BY A PERSON ═══
//
// Read from the cited envelope (extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope: housing 97.2 x 94.4 x 33 mm,
// housing edges +/-0.4, thickness +/-1) and the operator's caliper end face
// (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_end_face: face 97.84 x 38.60 mm) and the design's own wall and
// socket figures. A demand here that does not follow from those is a bug, not a preference.

// Delta's table gives housing length/depth as printed figures with no stated tolerance and a tolerance
// only on thickness (extdeps.fans.types RadialBlowerEnvelope has exactly these fields), so a face edge is
// the printed number and the thickness carries its +/-: the model bounds what the drawing bounds.
fn longest_face_edge(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { larger_i(a: um(m: b.housing_length), b: um(m: b.housing_depth)) }
fn shortest_face_edge(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { smaller_i(a: um(m: b.housing_length), b: um(m: b.housing_depth)) }
fn thickness_extent(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { um(m: b.thickness) + um(m: b.thickness_tolerance) }

// PLANE-ON-END: the thickness is all that goes back, so the operator's 70 mm buys it.
fn plane_on_end_run_back(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> Int { thickness_extent(b: b) + 2 * um(m: d.wall) }
// ...but its face spans the slot, where the pitch is the only room.
fn plane_on_end_slot_demand(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { longest_face_edge(b: b) }
// The same demand stated the way the SIDES are measured. The face spans the pitch, so what it spends
// into the region beside this card's own lane is the excess, halved: at 97.2 mm of housing over a 40.64 mm
// pitch that is 28.28 mm on each side. The operator's word about the sides ("70mm is fine") is a word
// about the space on ONE side, so this is the number it has to cover -- asking it against the whole 97.2
// would be asking a per-side allowance to cover a two-sided span.
fn plane_across_row_run_back(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> Int {
  blower_reach_behind(b: a16_blower_duct_design) + um(m: a16_blower_duct_design.socket_depth) + um(m: d.wall)
}
// ...and its long face runs along the card axis, so across the slot it spends only the thickness.
fn plane_across_row_slot_demand(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { thickness_extent(b: b) }

// OVER-THE-ROW: the fan clears the card's front face by the riser, so the chassis needs the card's own
// height across the face, plus the fan's vertical extent, plus the walls of the leg and the riser.
fn over_the_row_height(f: EndFaceMeasurement, d: GpuDuctDesign, b: RadialBlowerEnvelope, riser: Micrometer) -> Int {
  um(m: f.face_length) + um(m: riser) + thickness_extent(b: b) + 3 * um(m: d.wall)
}
// The leg behind the plug end is the only part of this home that spends the 70 mm: the fan's thickness
// turned on its side plus the turn's walls.
fn over_the_row_run_back(d: GpuDuctDesign, b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { thickness_extent(b: b) + 2 * um(m: d.wall) }
// And the fan's face still has to lie somewhere -- above the row it lies along the card row, so it is the
// ROW LENGTH that has to be free, and the card's own length is the first claim on it.
fn over_the_row_span(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Int { longest_face_edge(b: b) }

// BESIDE-THE-ROW: the fan sits out of the lane entirely; the lane has to take its shorter face.
fn beside_the_row_demand(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> Int { shortest_face_edge(b: b) + 2 * um(m: d.wall) + 2 * um(m: d.band_clearance) }

fn larger_i(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { if a > b { a } else { b } }
fn smaller_i(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { if a < b { a } else { b } }

// ═══ ADMISSION ═══

// ADMITTED and ADMITTED ON AN OPERATOR BOUND ARE NOT THE SAME VERDICT, so they are not the same shape.
// The first rests on a caliper reading: the chassis was measured and is bigger than the route needs. The
// second rests on a ruling: the operator authored a conservative bound and the route's need is under it,
// which is exactly what they said the bound was for. Only the second is available today. Both may print;
// what they do not both do is KNOW: a caliper admission carries a fact about the chassis, an operator-bound
// admission carries a decision about a design, and the difference has to survive in the shape or a declared
// 70 mm reads as a measured one. The margin each carries is measured against a different authority too --
// the reading in the first case, the bound in the second.
type RouteAdmission
  = RouteAdmitted { home: BlowerHome, needs: List<RouteDemand> }
  | RouteAdmittedOnOperatorBound { home: BlowerHome, needs: List<RouteDemand>, on_bound: ChassisGap, margin: Micrometer }
  | RouteProposalOnly { home: BlowerHome, needs: List<RouteDemand>, on_estimate: ChassisGap, margin: Micrometer }
  | RouteRefusedByChassis { home: BlowerHome, unmet: RouteDemand, gap: ChassisGap }
  | RouteHomeUnmeasured { home: BlowerHome, unmet: RouteDemand, gap: ChassisGap }

type RouteDemand { name: NonEmptyStr, needed: Micrometer, of_gap: NonEmptyStr }

fn demand_display(n: RouteDemand) -> String {
  join([n.name as String, " ", to_string(value: um(m: n.needed) / 1000), " mm (against the gap named ", n.of_gap as String, ")"], "")
}

fn route_admission_display(a: RouteAdmission) -> String {
  match a {
    RouteAdmitted {home: h, needs: ns} => join([blower_home_display(h: h), ": ADMITTED -- ", join(map(ns, n => demand_display(n: n)), "; ")], "")
    RouteAdmittedOnOperatorBound {home: h, needs: ns, on_bound: g, margin: mg} => join([
      blower_home_display(h: h), ": ADMITTED ON AN OPERATOR-DECLARED BOUND -- ", join(map(ns, n => demand_display(n: n)), "; "),
      "; needs ", to_string(value: um(m: mg) / 1000), " mm LESS than the bound allows; the bound is the operator's ruling, not a measurement (",
      gap_display(g: g), "); the print is admitted as a FIT PRINT on that ruling -- the part is the instrument that tests it"], "")
    RouteProposalOnly {home: h, needs: ns, on_estimate: g, margin: mg} => join([blower_home_display(h: h), ": PROPOSED ONLY -- ", join(map(ns, n => demand_display(n: n)), "; "), "; fits by ", to_string(value: um(m: mg) / 1000), " mm against an ESTIMATE (", gap_display(g: g), "); re-read before printing"], "")
    RouteRefusedByChassis {home: h, unmet: n, gap: g} => join([blower_home_display(h: h), ": REFUSED -- needs ", demand_display(n: n), " and the chassis gives ", gap_display(g: g)], "")
    RouteHomeUnmeasured {home: h, unmet: n, gap: g} => join([blower_home_display(h: h), ": REFUSED -- needs ", demand_display(n: n), " and that gap is UNMEASURED -- ", gap_display(g: g)], "")
  }
}

// THE ANSWER, FOR THE A16 ROW, AS RUN TEXT. Three homes, each with the reading it wants. A fourth card is
// another call with another end face; nothing here branches on the card's name.
fn a16_route_report() -> List<String> {
  [route_admission_display(a: admit_home(home: PlaneOnEnd)),
   route_admission_display(a: admit_home(home: PlaneAcrossRowStanding)),
   route_admission_display(a: admit_home(home: OverTheRow {riser: micrometer(count: 6000)})),
   route_admission_display(a: admit_home(home: BesideTheRow))]
}

fn a16_blower() -> RadialBlowerEnvelope { delta_bfb1012m_envelope }
fn a16_wall_design() -> GpuDuctDesign { a16_duct_design }

// THE PUBLISHED VERDICT: every home measured against THIS chassis -- the one gap the operator has given a
// word about, and the three that have never been read.
fn admit_home(home: BlowerHome) -> RouteAdmission {
  admit_home_over(home: home, rear: card_end_rear_fan_clearance, lane: neighbour_lane_width)
}

// PLANE-ON-END, over the two gaps it actually spends: what lies behind the plug end, and the lane beside
// this card's own lane that the housing's overhang reaches into. Split out from admit_home so a witness can
// run the SAME home against a chassis the operator has not blessed -- the refusal at a 30 mm rear is the
// same code path, with a different number in it, and not a hand-written verdict.
fn admit_plane_on_end(rear: ChassisGap, lane: ChassisGap) -> RouteAdmission {
  let b = a16_blower()
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  let home = PlaneOnEnd
  // The slot first: a 97 mm face at a 40.64 mm pitch is not a near thing, and it is not fixed by
  // re-reading the 70 mm. The run back is checked after, because it is the one the operator asked about.
  // What is asked of the neighbour lane is the OVERHANG per side, which is what the operator's word about
  // the sides was a word about; the pitch itself is the design's row geometry, not a chassis gap.
  let slot = RouteDemand { name: "across the slot (neighbour lane behind the ends)", needed: micrometer(count: plane_on_end_slot_demand(b: b)), of_gap: "lane between adjacent cards, behind the plug ends" }
  let overhang = RouteDemand { name: "overhang into the region beside this card's lane, per side", needed: plane_on_end_overhang_per_side(b: b), of_gap: "space on the sides of the card row" }
  match ask(gap: lane, need: um(m: overhang.needed), home: home, unmet: overhang) {
    Present { value: refused } => refused
    Absent => {
      let back = RouteDemand { name: "behind the plug end", needed: micrometer(count: plane_on_end_run_back(b: b, d: d)), of_gap: "plug end to rear chassis fan" }
      finish(home: home, needs: [slot, overhang, back], gap: rear, need: um(m: back.needed))
    }
  }
}

// THE SAME HOME, OVER THE GAPS YOU GIVE IT. The published verdict passes this chassis's rows; a witness
// passes a hypothetical one, so a refusal at 30 mm behind the end is the same rule with a different number
// in it rather than a hand-written conclusion.
fn admit_home_over(home: BlowerHome, rear: ChassisGap, lane: ChassisGap) -> RouteAdmission {
  let f = a16_end_face
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  let b = a16_blower()
  match home {
    PlaneOnEnd => admit_plane_on_end(rear: rear, lane: lane)
    PlaneAcrossRowStanding => {
      let back = RouteDemand { name: "behind the plug end", needed: micrometer(count: plane_across_row_run_back(b: b, d: d)), of_gap: "plug end to rear chassis fan" }
      match ask(gap: rear, need: um(m: back.needed), home: home, unmet: back) {
        Present { value: refused } => refused
        Absent => {
          let across = RouteDemand { name: "across the slot", needed: micrometer(count: plane_across_row_slot_demand(b: b)), of_gap: "lane between adjacent cards, behind the plug ends" }
          match ask(gap: lane, need: um(m: across.needed), home: home, unmet: across) {
            Present { value: refused2 } => refused2
            Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: home, needs: [back, across] }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    OverTheRow {riser: r} => {
      // Height first: it is the reading this home exists to need, and it has never been taken.
      let high = RouteDemand { name: "deck to lid above the row", needed: micrometer(count: over_the_row_height(f: f, d: d, b: b, riser: r)), of_gap: "deck to chassis lid, behind the plug end" }
      match ask(gap: deck_to_lid_clearance, need: um(m: high.needed), home: home, unmet: high) {
        Present { value: refused } => refused
        Absent => {
          let back = RouteDemand { name: "behind the plug end (the turned leg)", needed: micrometer(count: over_the_row_run_back(d: d, b: b)), of_gap: "plug end to rear chassis fan" }
          match ask(gap: rear, need: um(m: back.needed), home: home, unmet: back) {
            Present { value: refused2 } => refused2
            Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: home, needs: [high, back, RouteDemand { name: "fan face span along the row", needed: micrometer(count: over_the_row_span(b: b)), of_gap: "free row length behind the card ends" }] }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    BesideTheRow => {
      let beside = RouteDemand { name: "free lane beside the row", needed: micrometer(count: beside_the_row_demand(b: b, d: d)), of_gap: "free lane beside the card row" }
      match ask(gap: side_lane_width, need: um(m: beside.needed), home: home, unmet: beside) {
        Present { value: refused } => refused
        Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: home, needs: [beside] }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn ask(gap: ChassisGap, need: Int, home: BlowerHome, unmet: RouteDemand) -> RouteAdmission? {
  match gap {
    NeedsMeasurement {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, read_obligation: _} => Present { value: RouteHomeUnmeasured { home: home, unmet: unmet, gap: gap } }
    Measured {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, distance: dist, standing: _} =>
      // What the need is compared against. Three cases, and the middle one is the one that matters:
      //
      //   declared bound   the operator authored a limit the design may spend, so THAT is the number a need
      //                    is measured against -- not the row's `distance`, which would let the two figures
      //                    in the row drift apart with nobody noticing which one governed.
      //   a spoken estimate  the distance is what somebody thinks is there and the bound is how wrong they
      //                    might be, so the room a design may count on is `distance - bound`. Comparing a
      //                    need against the bare distance would spend a clearance the estimate itself says
      //                    may not exist -- and would read a "60 mm by eye" as a 60 mm guarantee, which is
      //                    exactly the laundering 4d forbids.
      //   a caliper reading  the distance is a fact about the chassis and the grain is in the instrument, so
      //                    the distance governs; a caliper's +/-0.05 mm is below every need here.
      let limit = if gap_is_estimate(g: gap) {
        match gap_declared_bound(g: gap) {
          Present { value: b } => um(m: dist) - um(m: b)
          Absent => um(m: dist)
        }
      } else {
        match gap_declared_bound(g: gap) { Present { value: b } => um(m: b) Absent => um(m: dist) }
      }
      if limit < need { Present { value: RouteRefusedByChassis { home: home, unmet: unmet, gap: gap } } } else { Absent }
  }
}

// THE ADMISSION RULE, IN ONE PLACE. A gap's standing decides what a route may do with it, and the three
// operator-facing standings differ in kind, not in degree:
//
//   caliper reading      the chassis was measured and is bigger than the route needs   -> ADMITTED
//   declared bound       the operator authored a conservative allowance, and the route's need is under it
//                        -> ADMITTED ON AN OPERATOR BOUND. This is the operator ruling of 2026-10-26 read
//                        literally: they did not measure 70 mm, they ALLOWED 70 mm (gunbc.design_ruling
//                        a16_rear_clearance_and_sides_are_operator_declared_bounds), and a design may be
//                        admitted on a limit the owner of the chassis set for it. The same ruling carries
//                        the FIT PRINT -- the operator answered "can we print" with those two figures, and
//                        the print is what catches a wrong one (chassis_clearances gap_may_be_printed_on).
//                        The bound still buys no MEASUREMENT: gap_is_measured is false and the reading is
//                        listed as owed, so nothing here mistakes a decision for an observation.
//   spoken estimate      a guess at what the gap is, which admits nothing -- a 70 mm that turns out to be
//                        64 mm is a cracked housing                                -> PROPOSED ONLY
//   unread               nobody has looked                                            -> REFUSED
fn finish(home: BlowerHome, needs: List<RouteDemand>, gap: ChassisGap, need: Int) -> RouteAdmission {
  match ask(gap: gap, need: need, home: home, unmet: RouteDemand { name: "final gap", needed: micrometer(count: need), of_gap: "final gap" }) {
    Present { value: refused } => refused
    Absent => match gap {
      Measured {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, distance: _, standing: DeclaredConservativeByOperator {bound: bnd, reading: _, date: _, positions: _, basis: _, does_not_settle: _}} => RouteAdmittedOnOperatorBound { home: home, needs: needs, on_bound: gap, margin: micrometer(count: um(m: bnd) - need) }
      Measured {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, distance: dist, standing: EstimatedByOperator {bound: bnd, date: _, positions: _, bound_is: _}} => RouteProposalOnly { home: home, needs: needs, on_estimate: gap, margin: micrometer(count: um(m: dist) - um(m: bnd) - need) }
      Measured {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, distance: _, standing: CaliperByOperator {instrument: _, date: _, positions: _}} => RouteAdmitted { home: home, needs: needs }
      NeedsMeasurement {name: _, from_point: _, to_point: _, read_obligation: _} => RouteHomeUnmeasured { home: home, unmet: RouteDemand { name: "final gap", needed: micrometer(count: need), of_gap: "final gap" }, gap: gap }
    }
  }
}

// ═══ THE ONE OBLIGATION THAT NO GEOMETRY HERE CAN CLOSE ═══
//
// The outlet has to line up with the cooler's window, and Delta's drawing does not attribute the outlet's
// position on the housing (extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_outlet_standing). Every home above assumes
// the outlet CAN be aimed at the window; none of them can prove it. The straight-back duct already lives
// with that ambiguity and says so (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct); these homes inherit the same caveat
// rather than inventing a check that would be a guess about where the fan blows.
fn outlet_alignment_obligation() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["operator or a clearer Delta drawing: where the outlet lies on the housing's periphery (its angle from the volute's tangent and its extent). Every home here needs the outlet aimed at the cooler window product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_end_face records; the model refuses to guess the angle."], "")
}

// ================================================================================================
// THE SIX READINGS OF 2026-10-27, RUN AGAINST EVERY HOME
// ================================================================================================
//
// The operator gave six rough figures on 2026-10-27 and a seventh fact that changes the row model:
//
//   "the plug latch is 3mm"                                        -- carried in chassis_clearances as
//                                                                     power_connector_latch
//   "between the two cards is 46mm"                                -- inter_card_gap
//   "the outer side of one card there are only a few mm til one edge of the chassis"
//                                                                  -- outer_side_at_the_wall, carried at ZERO
//   "The outer edge of the other card has 60mm until it touches the heatsink"
//                                                                  -- side_lane_width
//   "There is 70mm between each card to the front of the chassis fans."
//                                                                  -- card_to_rear_fans
//   "from floor to t he lid is 120mm"                              -- deck_to_lid_clearance
//
// and, in the same message, that THIS CHASSIS HOLDS TWO CARDS. That last one is not a dimension and does
// not get a gap row; it is recorded here, in the module that decides where a blower may sit, because it is
// the fact that makes the inter-card gap a binding constraint instead of a spare reading: with one card
// ducted, the housing's overhang goes into a neighbour lane that may be empty air; with two, BOTH ducts
// spend the same 46 mm, and what each may spend is half of what the gap leaves.
//
// WHAT THESE SIX ARE, AND WHAT THEY ARE NOT. Every one of them is EstimatedByOperator: a person looked and
// said a number, with no instrument, no named pair of points, and no repeat. None of them is
// CaliperByOperator and none is ObservedByReceipt, and none of them ADMITS a home -- the standing that
// admits is DeclaredConservativeByOperator, where the operator authored an allowance the design may spend
// (the 70 mm behind the plug end, and the sides). A reported "60 mm" is a report, not a permission. So the
// verdicts below are the honest ones: these readings do not lift a single home into admission, and the
// room they leave a design is the figure MINUS the width this repository puts on a by-eye number
// (chassis_clearances gap_room), which is what ask() does with an estimate.
//
// WHY THE VERDICTS DO NOT TURN ON THOSE WIDTHS. Each failure below is by 15 mm or more against a gap whose
// width is at most 10 mm, and several by tens of millimetres; none of them is close enough for the width
// to decide it. Saying so is part of the verdict -- "refused, and the estimate's +/- is not what refuses
// it" tells the operator whether to go measure something.

// A hypothetical planform, so the corpus can ask WHAT WOULD FIT instead of only whether THIS one does. Its
// name says what it is: a shape for a derivation, not a part anybody sells. The operator has since fixed the
// fan -- four Delta BFB1012M in hand, for every variant (2026-10-28) -- so no derivation below may assume a
// wheel a vendor happens to sell; the fan is extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope, and what varies is
// the duct wrapped around it.
data hypothetical_blower_shape: NonEmptyStr = "hypothetical planform; no vendor, no datasheet, nothing to buy; the fan itself is NOT hypothetical (extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope)"

// A micrometre figure for a demand or an extent, from a literal in the source. Literals stay literals;
// nothing measured, declared or reported travels as a bare number.
fn shape_um(m: Int) -> Micrometer { micrometer(count: m) }

// An extent of a planform: what the chassis leaves in one direction, WITH the kind of fact behind it.
//
// Three cases, because there are three different things a reader needs to distinguish and a single
// Micrometer plus a Bool cannot carry them:
//
//   Fits             readings cover this direction and a maximum follows from them. Carries the maximum.
//   FullySubscribed  readings cover it too, but what the duct must spend is more than the gap leaves: the
//                    home does not fit THIS direction, and by how much is a measured fact worth reporting.
//   Unread           no reading reaches this direction at all. Not zero, not negative: there is no number,
//                    and inventing one -- or smuggling absence in through a sign -- is the fabricated output
//                    DESIGN.md §5 refuses and gunbc.design_ruling dag_absence_is_one_meaning forbids.
//
// The old shape carried all three as one Micrometer whose sign meant "probably unmeasured", which merged the
// second case into the first and let a caller's arithmetic turn the third into a plausible-looking extent.
type ShapeExtent
  = Fits { at_most: Micrometer, capped_by: NonEmptyStr }
  | FullySubscribed { short_by: Micrometer, capped_by: NonEmptyStr }
  | Unread { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// The room a gap leaves, moved through arithmetic WITHOUT losing what kind of fact it is. Each of these
// takes and returns a GapRoom; none of them can be handed a made-up number, and none of them can answer for
// a direction nobody measured. The signed integer a subtraction needs exists only inside their bodies.
fn room_plus(base: GapRoom, room: GapRoom) -> GapRoom {
  match base {
    NoReading {obligation: o} => NoReading { obligation: o }
    OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: o} => match room {
      Room {room: r, from: _} =>
        let left = micrometer_count(m: r) - micrometer_count(m: s)
        if left < 0 { OverSubscribed { shortfall: micrometer(count: 0 - left), from: o } } else { Room { room: micrometer(count: left), from: o } }
      OverSubscribed {shortfall: s2, from: o2} => OverSubscribed { shortfall: micrometer(count: micrometer_count(m: s) + micrometer_count(m: s2)), from: o2 }
      NoReading {obligation: o2} => NoReading { obligation: o2 }
    }
    Room {room: b, from: o} => match room {
      Room {room: r, from: _} => Room { room: micrometer(count: micrometer_count(m: b) + micrometer_count(m: r)), from: o }
      OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: _} =>
        let left = micrometer_count(m: b) - micrometer_count(m: s)
        if left < 0 { OverSubscribed { shortfall: micrometer(count: 0 - left), from: o } } else { Room { room: micrometer(count: left), from: o } }
      NoReading {obligation: o2} => NoReading { obligation: o2 }
    }
  }
}

// A measured distance of the model itself (the slot pitch, drawn on NVIDIA's card drawing) entering the
// same arithmetic as a gap's room. This is NOT a chassis reading and never becomes one: it is a fact about
// the card, and the only reason it appears here is that a span across the card row is measured from the
// centre of one lane to the centre of the next.
fn room_of(m: Micrometer, from: NonEmptyStr) -> GapRoom { Room { room: m, from: from } }

fn room_less(room: GapRoom, deductions: List<Micrometer>) -> GapRoom {
  let taken = fold(deductions, init: 0, f: (t, w) => t + (micrometer_count(m: w) as Int))
  match room {
    Room {room: r, from: o} =>
      let left = (micrometer_count(m: r) as Int) - taken
      if left < 0 { OverSubscribed { shortfall: micrometer(count: 0 - left), from: o } } else { Room { room: micrometer(count: left), from: o } }
    OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: o} => OverSubscribed { shortfall: micrometer(count: (micrometer_count(m: s) as Int) + taken), from: o }
    NoReading {obligation: o} => NoReading { obligation: o }
  }
}

// A gap's room, reported as an extent, with the sentence the reader checks it back to. `less` is what the
// duct itself spends inside that room -- walls, clearances -- and `why` names the deductions in words.
fn extent_of(room: GapRoom, why: NonEmptyStr) -> ShapeExtent {
  match room {
    Room {room: r, from: o} => Fits { at_most: r, capped_by: join([o, "; ", why], "") }
    OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: o} => FullySubscribed { short_by: s, capped_by: join([o, "; ", why], "") }
    NoReading {obligation: o} => Unread { obligation: join(["no reading reaches this direction -- ", o], "") }
  }
}

// A direction the chassis simply was not measured in. Carries the obligation, never a number.
fn unread_extent(obligation: NonEmptyStr) -> ShapeExtent { Unread { obligation: join(["no reading reaches this direction -- ", obligation], "") } }

// THE LARGEST PLANFORM EACH HOME COULD TAKE, given what the chassis actually leaves. Every extent comes from
// a gap row and carries that row's provenance, so a reader can check any single number back to the
// operator's sentence that produced it; where nothing was read, the answer says so and names the reading
// owed, rather than reporting a maximum somebody never took.
type BlowerShape { across_the_binding_gap: ShapeExtent, along_the_row: ShapeExtent, behind_the_end: ShapeExtent }

fn shape_max_for_home(home: BlowerHome, d: GpuDuctDesign, riser: Micrometer) -> BlowerShape {
  let rear = gap_room(g: card_end_rear_fan_clearance)
  let walls_two = [d.wall, d.wall]
  match home {
    // BESIDE THE ROW. The fan lies flat in the free lane: its planform spans the lane, so the lane caps the
    // edge that crosses it, and the rear bound caps the edge that runs back behind the end. The fan's own
    // thickness stands up toward the lid, and nothing was read between the DECK and the lid -- the 120 mm is
    // floor-to-lid at an unnamed place, and no deck height exists -- so this home's third extent reports the
    // reading it still owes.
    BesideTheRow => BlowerShape {
      across_the_binding_gap: extent_of(room: room_less(room: gap_room(g: side_lane_width), deductions: concat(walls_two, [d.band_clearance, d.band_clearance])), why: "less two duct walls and the band clearance on both sides"),
      along_the_row: extent_of(room: room_less(room: rear, deductions: [d.sleeve_clearance]), why: "less the sleeve clearance"),
      behind_the_end: unread_extent(obligation: "nothing was read between the chassis deck and the lid at the height a fan standing on its side would need"),
    }
    // OVER THE ROW. The fan's thickness is what stands up, so the lid caps it directly; the card's own height
    // and the riser are inside the same 120 mm figure and are subtracted first. The other two extents run
    // along and across the row, where nothing was read.
    OverTheRow {riser: _} => BlowerShape {
      across_the_binding_gap: extent_of(room: room_less(room: gap_room(g: deck_to_lid_clearance), deductions: concat([a16_end_face.face_length, riser], [d.wall, d.wall, d.wall])), why: "less the card's own height, the riser, and the three walls a leg needs"),
      along_the_row: unread_extent(obligation: "nothing was read along the row, where this home's long edge would run"),
      behind_the_end: extent_of(room: rear, why: "the rear bound itself"),
    }
    // FLAT ON THE PLUG END, TWO DUCTED CARDS. The planform lies in the end-face plane: one edge across the
    // slot, the other up the card's height, the thickness behind the end. Across the slot the binding cap is
    // the narrower of two, both named (flat_on_end_housing_length_cap); up the card's height it may not
    // exceed the card itself without overhanging into the slot above, and the card's height is the only
    // reading there; behind the end the declared 70 mm governs and the 120 mm is not in this axis at all.
    PlaneOnEnd => BlowerShape {
      across_the_binding_gap: extent_of(room: flat_on_end_housing_length_cap(), why: "one slot pitch plus the declared side lane on each side -- the pair question is flat_on_end_twin_verdict, not this"),
      along_the_row: extent_of(room: room_of(m: a16_end_face.face_length, from: "the card's own end-face height (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_end_face)"), why: "beyond it the housing overhangs into the slot above, and no reading covers that"),
      behind_the_end: extent_of(room: room_less(room: rear, deductions: walls_two), why: "less the two duct walls the stack needs"),
    }
    // PLANE ACROSS THE ROW (today's duct): the slot bounds its long edge and the rear bound its reach.
    PlaneAcrossRowStanding => BlowerShape {
      across_the_binding_gap: extent_of(room: room_of(m: a16_slot_pitch(), from: "the 40.64 mm slot pitch"), why: "the housing may not overhang into a neighbouring card's lane"),
      along_the_row: unread_extent(obligation: "nothing was read along the row for this home"),
      behind_the_end: extent_of(room: room_less(room: rear, deductions: [d.wall]), why: "less the wall at the back"),
    }
  }
}

// The slot pitch as a length, not a count.
fn a16_slot_pitch() -> Micrometer { a16_blower_duct_design.slot_pitch }

// How far one housing's planform overhangs each side of its own lane: the part of its across-slot span that
// does not fit the pitch, halved, because the planform is centred on the slot.
fn plane_on_end_overhang_per_side(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Micrometer {
  let excess = longest_face_edge(b: b) - um(m: a16_slot_pitch())
  if excess <= 0 { micrometer(count: 0) } else { micrometer(count: excess / 2) }
}

// WHAT TWO DUCTED CARDS SPEND BETWEEN THEM. Two adjacent ducted cards put their overhangs facing each other
// across the clear gap between the cards, and the span they share is NOT the gap alone -- it is the gap PLUS
// one card's pitch, because each overhang starts at the far edge of its own lane. The tempting wrong
// arithmetic is to compare the pair's overhang against the 46 mm alone, which refuses a home that in fact
// clears; a model that over-forbids is as wrong as one that over-admits, so both sides of this comparison
// are derived, and flat_on_end_twin_verdict reports the margin rather than an opinion.
fn flat_on_end_pair_need(b: RadialBlowerEnvelope) -> Micrometer {
  let one = plane_on_end_overhang_per_side(b: b)
  micrometer(count: micrometer_count(m: one) * 2)
}

// The span the pair shares: one pitch, plus whatever the inter-card gap actually leaves. If the gap has no
// reading, the span has no reading -- the type says so instead of inventing a number.
fn flat_on_end_pair_span() -> GapRoom {
  room_plus(base: room_of(m: a16_slot_pitch(), from: "one 40.64 mm slot pitch, from the NVIDIA card drawing (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_blower_duct_design.slot_pitch)"),
            room: gap_room(g: inter_card_gap))
}

// The largest housing length ONE ducted card may span across the slot, on its own: the pitch of its lane
// plus whatever the operator's declared side lane lets it overhang on EACH side -- pitch + 2 * that lane.
// Deliberately NOT narrowed by the twin-card span: the pair question is its own verdict below, with its own
// number, because the two cards' overhangs face each other across the gap between them rather than each
// spending a full lane. Folding the pair span in here would forbid a single card from spending room that the
// twin case already shows it does not need -- over-forbidding, which DESIGN.md §4d refuses as plainly as it
// refuses fabricating. Any cap with no reading makes the answer unread: the unmeasured one is exactly the
// one that could be binding.
fn flat_on_end_housing_length_cap() -> GapRoom {
  let pitch = room_of(m: a16_slot_pitch(), from: "one 40.64 mm slot pitch, from the NVIDIA card drawing (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_blower_duct_design.slot_pitch)")
  room_plus(base: room_plus(base: pitch, room: gap_room(g: neighbour_lane_width)), room: gap_room(g: neighbour_lane_width))
}

// Does the pair clear? Reported with its margin, because the margin is the point, and with the kind of each
// number kept: a span that has no reading cannot be called clear or refused.
fn flat_on_end_twin_verdict() -> String {
  let need = plane_on_end_pair_need_for_blower()
  match flat_on_end_pair_span() {
    NoReading {obligation: o} => join(["two ducted cards, flat on the end: the span they share has no reading -- ", o], "")
    Room {room: span, from: o} =>
      let margin = micrometer_count(m: span) - micrometer_count(m: need)
      if margin < 0 {
        join(["two ducted cards, flat on the end: both housings' overhang is ", mm_text(m: need),
              " against a shared span of ", mm_text(m: span), " (", o, ") -- REFUSED, short by ",
              to_string(value: 0 - margin / 1000), " mm"], "")
      } else {
        join(["two ducted cards, flat on the end: both housings' overhang is ", mm_text(m: need),
              " against a shared span of ", mm_text(m: span), " (", o, ") -- clear, with ",
              to_string(value: margin / 1000), " mm to spare"], "")
      }
    OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: o} => join(["two ducted cards, flat on the end: the inter-card gap is over-subscribed before the housings even start -- ", mm_text(m: s), " gone (", o, ")"], "")
  }
}

fn plane_on_end_pair_need_for_blower() -> Micrometer { flat_on_end_pair_need(b: a16_blower()) }

fn mm_text(m: Micrometer) -> String { join([to_string(value: micrometer_count(m: m) / 1000), " mm"], "") }

fn extent_display(e: ShapeExtent) -> String {
  match e {
    Fits {at_most: a, capped_by: c} => join(["at most ", mm_text(m: a), " (", c, ")"], "")
    FullySubscribed {short_by: s, capped_by: c} => join(["NOTHING FITS -- short by ", mm_text(m: s), " (", c, ")"], "")
    Unread {obligation: o} => o
  }
}

fn shape_display(s: BlowerShape) -> String {
  join(["the edge across the binding gap ", extent_display(e: s.across_the_binding_gap),
        "; the edge along the row ", extent_display(e: s.along_the_row),
        "; behind the end ", extent_display(e: s.behind_the_end)], "")
}

// THE VERDICT PER HOME, AS RUN TEXT, against the 2026-10-27 readings. Not a re-derivation by hand: every
// line runs the same ask()/finish() the published verdicts use, with the operator's own gap rows passed in
// as the gaps. The flat-on-end line is the twin case -- the same home, with the inter-card gap standing in
// for the generous side, which is what two ducted cards in one row do to each other.
fn verdict_against_2026_10_27_readings() -> List<String> {
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  let b = a16_blower()
  let lane_need = beside_the_row_demand(b: b, d: d)
  let lane = RouteDemand { name: "housing width across the free lane", needed: shape_um(m: lane_need), of_gap: "free lane beside the card row" }
  let at_lane = ask(gap: side_lane_width, need: lane_need, home: BesideTheRow, unmet: lane)
  let at_wall = ask(gap: outer_side_at_the_wall, need: lane_need, home: BesideTheRow, unmet: lane)
  let lid_need = over_the_row_height(f: a16_end_face, d: d, b: b, riser: shape_um(m: 6000))
  let lid = ask(gap: deck_to_lid_clearance, need: lid_need, home: OverTheRow {riser: shape_um(m: 6000)}, unmet: RouteDemand { name: "deck to lid above the row", needed: shape_um(m: lid_need), of_gap: "deck to chassis lid, behind the plug end" })
  let pair_need = micrometer_count(m: flat_on_end_pair_need(b: b))
  let twins = ask(gap: inter_card_gap, need: pair_need, home: PlaneOnEnd, unmet: RouteDemand { name: "both housings' overhang, across the gap between the two cards", needed: shape_um(m: pair_need), of_gap: "clear gap between the two cards" })
  [route_admission_display(a: match at_lane { Present { value: r } => r Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: BesideTheRow, needs: [lane] } }),
   route_admission_display(a: match at_wall { Present { value: r } => r Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: BesideTheRow, needs: [lane] } }),
   route_admission_display(a: match lid { Present { value: r } => r Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: OverTheRow {riser: shape_um(m: 6000)}, needs: [] } }),
   route_admission_display(a: match twins { Present { value: r } => r Absent => RouteAdmitted { home: PlaneOnEnd, needs: [] } }),
   flat_on_end_twin_verdict(),
   "and the one home the operator declared a bound for, unchanged by any of this: ",
   route_admission_display(a: admit_home(home: PlaneOnEnd))]
}

// THE ANSWER TO "WHICH BLOWER WOULD FIT": the largest planform each home can take under the 2026-10-27
// readings, with the kind of every number attached -- a maximum, a shortfall, or the reading still owed.
fn largest_planform_per_home() -> List<String> {
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  let b = a16_blower()
  [join(["beside the row, against the '60mm' side: ", shape_display(s: shape_max_for_home(home: BesideTheRow, d: d, riser: shape_um(m: 6000)))], ""),
   join(["over the row on a 6 mm riser: ", shape_display(s: shape_max_for_home(home: OverTheRow {riser: shape_um(m: 6000)}, d: d, riser: shape_um(m: 6000)))], ""),
   join(["flat on the plug end, two ducted cards across the inter-card gap: ", shape_display(s: shape_max_for_home(home: PlaneOnEnd, d: d, riser: shape_um(m: 6000)))], ""),
   join(["what the BFB1012M actually asks for: across the slot ", mm_text(m: micrometer(count: longest_face_edge(b: b))),
         "; up the end face ", mm_text(m: micrometer(count: shortest_face_edge(b: b))),
         "; behind the end ", mm_text(m: micrometer(count: plane_on_end_run_back(b: b, d: d))), " (fan envelope from extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope, cited)"], "")]
}

// THE FIXED FAN DECIDES THE POSE, AND THE POSE DECIDES WHETHER IT FITS
//
// The fan was fixed by the operator's ruling of 2026-10-28 (relayed by wise-bat-528): the Delta BFB1012M,
// four on hand, for every variant. That retires any "largest wheel that fits": a wheel you may choose is a
// different object from a wheel you already own, and sizing a hypothetical one answers a question nobody is
// asking. What the reference image still asks IS answerable, and from cited dimensions alone -- but only once
// the axes are named. This module's frame (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct plane_on_end_duct_parts) is:
//
//   x  along the card's END FACE, the 97.84 mm bracket face (gpu_duct a16_end_face face_length)
//   y  ACROSS THE ROW -- where the 46 mm between the cards and the 60 mm heatsink lane live
//   z  FORE-AFT, the direction anything goes BEHIND THE PLUG END -- the declared 70 mm lives here
//
// The BFB1012M's datasheet row (extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope, standing TranscribedUncited)
// says which of its three dimensions is which: housing_length 97.2 mm runs ALONG THE FLOW PATH, housing_depth
// 94.4 mm ACROSS it, and the 33 mm thickness IS THE WHEEL AXIS -- the only direction a double-inlet radial
// takes air along ("Both inlets face along the axis, directly opposite each other"). So a pose is nothing
// more mysterious than WHICH MODEL AXIS EACH ENVELOPE DIMENSION LIES ALONG, and there are exactly six:
// three choices of where the 33 mm axis goes, times two spins about it. All six are enumerated below and
// all six are checked against the readings; none is hand-picked, and the answer is a derived fact rather
// than a claim. Each direction is a typed axis, not a sentence (DESIGN.md SS4c: a fact belongs in a carrier;
// SS2: a String leaf hiding named parts is anemic modeling), and the display is a separate reading-out.
type EnvelopeAxis
  = AlongEndFace     // x: along the 97.84 mm bracket face
  | AcrossRow        // y: across the card row
  | ForeAft          // z: behind the plug end

fn envelope_axis_display(a: EnvelopeAxis) -> String {
  match a {
    AlongEndFace => "along the end face, horizontal (x)"
    AcrossRow => "across the card row, toward the neighbour card or the heatsink (y)"
    ForeAft => "fore-aft, out of the plug end, parallel to the PCB (z)"
  }
}

// A fan dimension placed on an axis. Two of these and the axis the wheel stands on describe any pose.
type PlacedDimension {
  along: EnvelopeAxis,
  extent: Micrometer,
  which_envelope_dimension: NonEmptyStr,   // names the cited row's field, so the number has a source
}

fn placed(along: EnvelopeAxis, extent: Micrometer, which_envelope_dimension: NonEmptyStr) -> PlacedDimension {
  PlacedDimension { along: along, extent: extent, which_envelope_dimension: which_envelope_dimension }
}

// The same dimension as it stands INSIDE the printed pocket: the housing always sits in a pocket, so two
// walls are always added to whatever goes behind the end. This is the pocket, not a guess at one -- the same
// `wall` the duct design carries.
fn placed_in_pocket(p: PlacedDimension, wall: Micrometer) -> PlacedDimension {
  placed(along: p.along, extent: micrometer(count: micrometer_count(m: p.extent) + 2 * micrometer_count(m: wall)), which_envelope_dimension: p.which_envelope_dimension)
}

// What one pose spends along one axis, and whether the room there covers it. Room keeps its kind and origin
// inside ShapeExtent; `clears` is the one bit of arithmetic, and an unread direction never reports as clear.
type AxisCheck {
  along: EnvelopeAxis,
  need: Micrometer,
  room: ShapeExtent,
  clears: Bool,
}

// A direction that HAS a room is not automatically a direction that CLEARS: the room is an allowance and
// the need is a claim on it, and when the claim exceeds the allowance the honest report is
// FullySubscribed with the difference, not Fits with a false flag. Reporting Fits there would let every
// renderer downstream print "at most 70 mm" beside a 101 mm part as though the two agreed.
fn axis_check(along: EnvelopeAxis, need: Micrometer, room: GapRoom) -> AxisCheck {
  match room {
    Room {room: r, from: o} =>
      let spare = (micrometer_count(m: r) as Int) - (micrometer_count(m: need) as Int)
      if spare < 0 {
        AxisCheck { along: along, need: need, room: FullySubscribed { short_by: micrometer(count: 0 - spare), capped_by: o }, clears: false }
      } else {
        AxisCheck { along: along, need: need, room: Fits { at_most: r, capped_by: o }, clears: true }
      }
    OverSubscribed {shortfall: s, from: o} => AxisCheck { along: along, need: need, room: FullySubscribed { short_by: s, capped_by: o }, clears: false }
    NoReading {obligation: o} => AxisCheck { along: along, need: need, room: Unread { obligation: o }, clears: false }
  }
}

// A pose of the fixed fan: which way its wheel axis points, and what that puts on each model axis.
type FanPose {
  wheel_axis: EnvelopeAxis,
  behind_end: PlacedDimension,
  across_row: PlacedDimension,
  along_end_face: PlacedDimension,
}

// A pose's three checks, as NAMED fields rather than a positional List. A list read with [i] yields an
// Optional -- the substrate refuses to invent an element for an index past the end -- and a pose always
// has exactly these three directions, so making them three fields says so and removes a whole class of
// unwrap. It also means a caller cannot silently take the across-row room when it meant the rear room.
type PoseFit {
  rear: AxisCheck,
  across_row: AxisCheck,
  end_face: AxisCheck,
}

// The six poses, enumerated rather than listed by hand: for each of the three axes the wheel could lie along,
// the two remaining envelope dimensions can be swapped between the other two axes. `wheels` is how far the
// printed collector's two walls add behind the end -- spent in every pose, because the housing always sits in
// a printed pocket.
fn fan_poses(fan: RadialBlowerEnvelope, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> List<FanPose> {
  let len = placed(along: ForeAft, extent: fan.housing_length, which_envelope_dimension: "housing_length (flow path), extdeps.fans.delta delta_bfb1012m_envelope")
  let dep = placed(along: ForeAft, extent: fan.housing_depth, which_envelope_dimension: "housing_depth (across the flow path), same row")
  let thk = placed(along: ForeAft, extent: fan.thickness, which_envelope_dimension: "thickness (the wheel axis), same row")
  let pocket = placed_in_pocket(p: thk, wall: d.wall)
  // wheel axis fore-aft: the printed home -- thickness behind the end, the disc standing in the x-y plane
  let upright_long_across = FanPose { wheel_axis: ForeAft, behind_end: pocket, across_row: len, along_end_face: dep }
  let upright_deep_across = FanPose { wheel_axis: ForeAft, behind_end: pocket, across_row: dep, along_end_face: len }
  // wheel axis across the row: THE REFERENCE IMAGE'S POSE (axis perpendicular to the card face). The disc
  // stands in the x-z plane, so a disc dimension is what recedes behind the end.
  let sideways_flat_long_behind = FanPose { wheel_axis: AcrossRow, behind_end: placed_in_pocket(p: len, wall: d.wall), across_row: thk, along_end_face: dep }
  let sideways_flat_deep_behind = FanPose { wheel_axis: AcrossRow, behind_end: placed_in_pocket(p: dep, wall: d.wall), across_row: thk, along_end_face: len }
  // wheel axis along the end face: the disc stands in the y-z plane; also a sideways intake, spun the other way
  let facewise_long_behind = FanPose { wheel_axis: AlongEndFace, behind_end: placed_in_pocket(p: len, wall: d.wall), across_row: dep, along_end_face: thk }
  let facewise_deep_behind = FanPose { wheel_axis: AlongEndFace, behind_end: placed_in_pocket(p: dep, wall: d.wall), across_row: len, along_end_face: thk }
  [ upright_long_across, upright_deep_across, sideways_flat_long_behind, sideways_flat_deep_behind, facewise_long_behind, facewise_deep_behind ]
}

// One pose against the three readings that can refuse it. The rear bound is the operator's declared 70 mm;
// across the row is the span the row leaves (one pitch plus the declared side lane on each side); along the
// end face is the card's own 97.84 mm bracket face, which is all the duct can be supported by. No room is
// read off a raw number -- each carries its standing's origin through gap_room.
fn fan_pose_checks(pose: FanPose, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> PoseFit {
  PoseFit {
    rear: axis_check(along: ForeAft, need: pose.behind_end.extent, room: gap_room(g: card_end_rear_fan_clearance)),
    across_row: axis_check(along: AcrossRow, need: pose.across_row.extent, room: flat_on_end_housing_length_cap()),
    end_face: axis_check(along: AlongEndFace, need: pose.along_end_face.extent, room: room_of(m: a16_end_face.face_length, from: "the card's own end-face length, caliper-read (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct a16_end_face)")),
  }
}

fn fan_pose_admits(fit: PoseFit) -> Bool { fit.rear.clears && fit.across_row.clears && fit.end_face.clears }

// Which of the six admit, as facts a caller can branch on.
fn admitting_fan_poses() -> List<FanPose> {
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  filter(fan_poses(fan: a16_blower(), d: d), p => fan_pose_admits(fit: fan_pose_checks(pose: p, d: d)))
}

// The printed home IS one of the six, and this says which: the duct's own run-back, from the same fn the CAD
// is built with, so a report can never drift from the geometry it describes.
fn printed_pose() -> FanPose {
  let fan = a16_blower()
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  let walls = micrometer(count: 2 * micrometer_count(m: d.wall))
  FanPose {
    wheel_axis: ForeAft,
    behind_end: placed(along: ForeAft, extent: plane_on_end_duct_run_back(b: a16_blower_duct_design, d: d), which_envelope_dimension: "thickness + tolerance + the printed pocket's two walls (gpu_duct plane_on_end_duct_run_back)"),
    across_row: placed(along: AcrossRow, extent: fan.housing_length, which_envelope_dimension: "housing_length, placed across the row by gpu_duct plane_on_end_duct_parts (housing_y = the larger envelope dimension)"),
    along_end_face: placed(along: AlongEndFace, extent: fan.housing_depth, which_envelope_dimension: "housing_depth, placed along the end face by the same fn (housing_x = the smaller)"),
  }
}

// The report's rendering -- the only place a pose becomes a sentence, and it reads out facts, adding nothing.
fn fan_pose_display(pose: FanPose, d: GpuDuctDesign) -> String {
  let fit = fan_pose_checks(pose: pose, d: d)
  join([if fan_pose_admits(fit: fit) { "ADMITTED" } else { "REFUSED" }, ": wheel axis ",
        envelope_axis_display(a: pose.wheel_axis),
        "; behind the end ", mm_text(m: pose.behind_end.extent), " (", pose.behind_end.which_envelope_dimension, ") vs ", extent_display(e: fit.rear.room),
        "; across the row ", mm_text(m: pose.across_row.extent), " (", pose.across_row.which_envelope_dimension, ") vs ", extent_display(e: fit.across_row.room),
        "; along the end face ", mm_text(m: pose.along_end_face.extent), " (", pose.along_end_face.which_envelope_dimension, ") vs ", extent_display(e: fit.end_face.room)], "")
}

fn fan_pose_displays() -> List<String> {
  let d = a16_wall_design()
  map(fan_poses(fan: a16_blower(), d: d), p => fan_pose_display(pose: p, d: d))
}

// ARE THE SCROLL AND THE FLAT-ON-END DUCT THE SAME GEOMETRY? ONE GEOMETRY, NOT TWO -- derived, above. The
// reference image's pose has its wheel axis perpendicular to the CARD FACE (across the row), which puts a DISC
// dimension behind the plug end: 94.4 mm, or 97.2 mm spun the other way, against the operator's declared
// 70 mm -- short by 28.4 mm at best, printed pocket walls included (best_sideways_rear_shortfall derives it). The poses that clear are the
// ones with the 33 mm wheel axis fore-aft, and that is the pose the flat-on-end duct already prints. What the
// image still contributes is the collector's SHAPE -- a throat that turns the wheel's tangential discharge
// into the heatsink's end face -- printed here as the duct's open-shoulder collector rather than as a second
// root. A second root would be the same plate under another name, and the operator asked for one to prepare.
fn scroll_is_not_a_second_geometry() -> String {
  join(["one geometry, not two. Of the ", to_string(value: length(list: fan_poses(fan: a16_blower(), d: a16_wall_design()))),
        " poses the fixed BFB1012M can stand in, ", to_string(value: length(list: admitting_fan_poses())),
        " clear every reading, and all of them have the 33 mm wheel axis fore-aft -- the pose gpu_duct plane_on_end_duct_parts already prints. The reference image's pose (axis across the row) is refused on cited envelope dimensions: a disc dimension goes behind the plug end and is short by ",
        mm_text(m: micrometer(count: best_sideways_rear_shortfall())), " at best. What survives of the image is its collector SHAPE, printed as the duct's open shoulder."], "")
}

// How far a pose falls short behind the end, as a positive Micrometer the reader never has to subtract.
// Refusal arithmetic, not fabrication: a pose that clears, or one whose room no reading reaches, has no
// shortfall to report and says so with zero rather than with a made-up number.
fn fan_pose_rear_shortfall(pose: FanPose) -> Micrometer {
  let check = fan_pose_checks(pose: pose, d: a16_wall_design()).rear
  match check.room {
    FullySubscribed {short_by: s, capped_by: _} => s
    Fits {at_most: _, capped_by: _} => micrometer(count: 0)
    Unread {obligation: _} => micrometer(count: 0)
  }
}

// The best the reference image's family of poses can do behind the plug end: the SMALLEST shortfall among the
// four sideways poses, which is the most favourable reading anyone could honestly give the idea.
fn best_sideways_rear_shortfall() -> Micrometer {
  let shortfalls = map(sideways_fan_poses(), p => micrometer_count(m: fan_pose_rear_shortfall(pose: p)))
  micrometer(count: fold(shortfalls, init: 0, f: (best, s) => closer_to_zero(best: best, other: s)))
}

// The smaller NON-ZERO shortfall: zero means "nothing to report" for a pose that clears or whose room no
// reading reaches, and it must never win the comparison and make the idea look better than it is.
fn closer_to_zero(best: Int, other: Int) -> Int {
  if other <= 0 { best }
  else if best == 0 { other }
  else if other < best { other }
  else { best }
}

// The four poses with the wheel axis perpendicular to the CARD FACE -- the reference image's family.
fn sideways_fan_poses() -> List<FanPose> {
  filter(fan_poses(fan: a16_blower(), d: a16_wall_design()), p => match p.wheel_axis { AcrossRow => true AlongEndFace => true ForeAft => false })
}

// WHERE EACH CARD'S AIR COMES FROM, per card, for the pose that actually admits.
//
// This is read off the model's OWN axes, which are: x along the end face's length (the 97.84 mm face),
// y across the row (where the 46 mm between the cards and the 60 mm heatsink lane live), and z fore-aft --
// the direction anything goes BEHIND the plug end. The duct puts the housing at 94.4 mm in x, 97.2 mm in y
// and 33 mm in z (product.printed_chassis.gpu_duct plane_on_end_duct_parts: housing_x = the smaller housing
// dimension, housing_y = the larger, th = the thickness). So the WHEEL AXIS LIES ALONG z: fore-aft, pointing
// OUT OF THE PLUG END, parallel to the PCB and perpendicular to the end face. A double-inlet radial takes air
// in along that axis, so the fan drinks from the space BEHIND THE PLUG END -- the 70 mm the operator read to
// the rear chassis fans -- and its discharge leaves tangentially, in the x-y plane, which is where the
// printed shoulder turns it down the window. The reference image's pose (axis along y, perpendicular to the
// card face) is one of the four refused by the fan's own dimensions above.
//
// WHY THIS IS A COPRODUCT AND NOT A STRING (DESIGN.md SS5: "every path succeeds fully or fails with a typed,
// located diagnostic"; SS2: "A String leaf hiding named parts is anemic modeling"). Two of the four cases
// below are REFUSALS -- the row has no cards, or the index is not a card -- and a caller must be able to tell
// a refusal from an answer without comparing sentences. And the answer itself names a direction, a bound and
// two obstacles, each of which is a fact with a standing; carrying them as English would put the operator's
// measurements and the geometry's conclusion in the same untyped blob, so a witness could only grep them.
// Every bound here is a ChassisGap: the carrier that already holds the measurement, its Micrometer, its
// standing and the operator's own words. The quotes live on those rows and on gunbc.design_ruling, once each.
// What stands where the air would otherwise come from, with the gap that measures it.
type BlockedSide
  = NeighbourCard { across: ChassisGap }   // the card on the other side of the inter-card gap
  | ChassisWall { at: ChassisGap }         // the chassis edge on the tight side of the row

// One card's inlet, or why there isn't one. The axis is the SAME EnvelopeAxis the poses above use -- an
// intake direction and a pose direction are the same kind of fact, and two enums for one fact is the
// re-invention DESIGN.md SS2 refuses.
type CardIntake
  = Facing {
      card_index: Int,
      wheel_axis: EnvelopeAxis,              // fore-aft, out of the plug end, for the admitted pose
      air_drawn_from: ChassisGap,            // the gap the inlet looks into
      replacement_air_from: ChassisGap,      // the lane air arrives through to replace it
      blocked: List<BlockedSide>,            // what it cannot drink through, and by which gap
    }
  | RowEmpty { cards: Int }                  // no card to face: the count is the diagnostic
  | NoSuchCard { index: Int, count: Int }

// The pose that admits has its axis fore-aft; the four refused ones put it sideways, where the fan's own
// datasheet dimensions refuse to fit. See fan_poses for the arithmetic.
fn admitted_wheel_axis() -> EnvelopeAxis { ForeAft }

fn card_intake(card_index: Int, card_count: Int) -> CardIntake {
  if card_count <= 0 {
    RowEmpty { cards: card_count }
  } else if card_index < 0 || card_index >= card_count {
    NoSuchCard { index: card_index, count: card_count }
  } else if card_count == 1 {
    // One card: nothing beside it, so both horizontal sides are chassis walls, and the supply is the space
    // behind the end with replacement air drawn down the lane the operator read on the free side.
    Facing {
      card_index: card_index, wheel_axis: admitted_wheel_axis(),
      air_drawn_from: card_to_rear_fans(), replacement_air_from: side_lane_width(),
      blocked: [ChassisWall { at: outer_side_at_the_wall() }],
    }
  } else if card_index == 0 {
    // The card nearer the tight wall: it cannot draw through the card beside it, and its other side is the
    // wall with "only a few mm" on it, so it drinks fore-aft like every other card here.
    Facing {
      card_index: card_index, wheel_axis: admitted_wheel_axis(),
      air_drawn_from: card_to_rear_fans(), replacement_air_from: side_lane_width(),
      blocked: [NeighbourCard { across: inter_card_gap() }, ChassisWall { at: outer_side_at_the_wall() }],
    }
  } else {
    Facing {
      card_index: card_index, wheel_axis: admitted_wheel_axis(),
      air_drawn_from: card_to_rear_fans(), replacement_air_from: side_lane_width(),
      blocked: [NeighbourCard { across: inter_card_gap() }],
    }
  }
}

// The report's rendering, which is the ONLY place this becomes a sentence. Facts stay above; this only reads
// them out, using gap_display so a bound always arrives with the standing it carries and the operator's words.
fn card_intake_display(c: CardIntake) -> String {
  match c {
    RowEmpty {cards: n} => join(["no cards in the row (count ", to_string(value: n), "): nothing to face"], "")
    NoSuchCard {index: i, count: n} => join(["no such card: index ", to_string(value: i), " of ", to_string(value: n)], "")
    Facing {card_index: i, wheel_axis: ax, air_drawn_from: air, replacement_air_from: lane, blocked: bs} => join([
      "card ", to_string(value: i), ": intake along ", envelope_axis_display(a: ax),
      "; drawn from ", gap_display(g: air), "; replacement air from ", gap_display(g: lane),
      "; cannot draw through ", join(map(bs, side_display), "; ")], "")
  }
}

fn side_display(s: BlockedSide) -> String {
  match s {
    NeighbourCard {across: g} => join(["the neighbour card across ", gap_display(g: g)], "")
    ChassisWall {at: g} => join(["the chassis wall at ", gap_display(g: g)], "")
  }
}

// ================================================================================================
// THE AIRFLOW THE A16 NEEDS -- AND WHY IT IS A FUNCTION AND NOT A NUMBER
// ================================================================================================
//
// The board is PassiveThermalSolution (extdeps.gpu.nvidia_a16 nvidia_a16_board_facts.thermal): it carries no
// fan of its own, so whatever moves its heat is THIS design's job. What the datasheet row does NOT carry is
// an airflow requirement -- line 23 of that module says so in as many words ("no airflow requirement"; the
// airflow-direction row states only which way the heat sink can be blown through). So a CFM figure written
// here as if it were transcribed would be invented, and that is the exact move 4d forbids.
//
// What CAN be derived is the flow that carries the board's own cited power away, given a temperature rise
// somebody is willing to accept. Q = m_dot * cp * dT, with m_dot from the density and specific heat the
// corpus already cites (extdeps.physics.dry_air dry_air_at_20c), inverted for the volume flow. The board
// power is cited (250 W default and maximum, 235 W MaxQ, 195 W minimum); cp and density are cited; the
// ALLOWABLE RISE is neither -- it is the operator's decision, so it is an argument and the answer is given
// as a curve over it, not a single number. The obligation is named below so nobody reads the curve as settled.
//
// Two things this leaves OUT, both honest rather than convenient: it assumes all the board's heat goes into
// the air this duct moves, which ignores the conduction into the board and the radiation off the fins that
// exhaust_thermal lists as unmodelled couplings, and it is free-air flow at the fan's inlet, which is not
// the flow the scroll will actually deliver against the scroll's own pressure drop. Both push the required
// free-air figure UP, never down, so reading these numbers as a minimum is the safe direction to read them.

// Volume flow needed to carry `heat` away at a rise of `rise`, using only cited properties:
// rho [g/m3] * V [mL/s] / 1000 = m_dot [mg/s], and Q [W] = m_dot [mg/s] * 1e-6 [kg/mg] * cp * dT. Inverted
// for V and rounded UP, because a flow rounded down under-delivers. Heat arrives as a Watt and the rise as a
// CelsiusDelta, and the answer is a MillilitrePerSecond -- the same carriers exhaust_thermal takes in and the
// fan rows carry, so nothing here unwraps a typed fact into a bare number only to wrap it again. The one
// place a bare Int is crossed is the Nat the carrier wants, and that crossing is made through
// std.checked_arithmetic.checked_int_to_nat, which refuses an impossible count rather than fabricating one:
// Absent here means the inputs asked for a flow that cannot be named.
// feature:missing-volume-flow-carrier-in-imperial -- dissolve-on: std.measure gains a cubic-foot-per-minute
// volume-flow carrier; until then mL/s is the corpus's only volumetric-flow authority (extdeps.gpu
// nvidia_passive_rows says so in as many words: convert CFM to mL/s when transcribing, never store the raw
// number under the field's name), and CFM exists below only as a rendering of it.
fn required_flow(heat: Watt, rise: CelsiusDelta) -> MillilitrePerSecond? {
  let rho = gram_per_cubic_metre_count(d: dry_air_at_20c.density)
  let cp = joule_per_kilogram_kelvin_count(c: dry_air_at_20c.specific_heat_cp)
  let denom = rho * cp * celsius_delta_count(delta: rise) / 1000
  if denom <= 0 { Absent } else {
    match checked_int_to_nat(n: (watt_count(w: heat) * 1000000 + denom - 1) / denom) {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: n } => Present { value: millilitre_per_second(count: n) }
    }
  }
}

// The same, checked back through the corpus's own forward function: exhaust_rise at the flow required_flow
// returns must give a rise no greater than asked. Carriers pass straight through -- the check cannot compare
// a number against a unit it silently dropped, which is the whole reason this is written as a round trip
// rather than as arithmetic on both sides.
fn required_flow_rise_check(heat: Watt, rise: CelsiusDelta) -> Bool {
  match required_flow(heat: heat, rise: rise) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: flow } => match exhaust_rise(heat: heat, flow: flow) {
      Present { value: d } => celsius_delta_count(delta: d) <= celsius_delta_count(delta: rise)
      Absent => false
    }
  }
}

// CFM as a RENDERING of a volumetric-flow carrier, never as a second storage of the same fact. 1 CFM =
// 471.9474 mL/s. Two decimal places, computed in integers: the count is carried in hundredths of a cubic
// foot per minute and split into digits here, so a reader gets "29.19 CFM" and nothing else in the corpus
// holds a CFM figure that could disagree with the mL/s it came from. The witness checks this rendering
// against the BFB1012M listing, which prints 22.60 CFM beside 10666 mL/s -- the one place the corpus has a
// published pair to check the conversion against.
fn free_air_cfm_text(flow: MillilitrePerSecond) -> String {
  let hundredths = (millilitre_per_second_count(f: flow) * 1000000 + 4719474 / 2) / 4719474
  let whole = hundredths / 100
  let cents = hundredths - whole * 100
  join([to_string(value: whole), ".", if cents < 10 { join(["0", to_string(value: cents)], "") } else { to_string(value: cents) }, " CFM"], "")
}

// The A16's own power rows, read rather than quoted: the default and the maximum are what the duct must be
// sized against, MaxQ and the minimum are what it is sized against when the operator caps the card.
fn a16_power_points_w() -> List<String> {
  // board_power is an Optional<GpuBoardPowerSpecification>: a board either states its power window or it
  // does not, and a report that printed "0 W" for the silent case would be the fabricated output DESIGN.md
  // SS5 refuses. So the absence is reported as absence.
  match nvidia_a16_board_facts.board_power {
    Absent => ["the A16's board row states no power window: nothing to size against"]
    Present { value: ConfigurableBoardPower { default: dd, minimum: lo, maximum: hi, named_points: _ } } =>
      [join(["default ", to_string(value: watt_count(w: dd)), " W"], ""),
       join(["maximum ", to_string(value: watt_count(w: hi)), " W"], ""),
       join(["minimum ", to_string(value: watt_count(w: lo)), " W"], "")]
    Present { value: RatedBoardPower { rated: r } } =>
      [join(["rated ", to_string(value: watt_count(w: r)), " W (the vendor states one figure, not a window)"], "")]
  }
}

// THE CURVE, at the board's maximum, over the rises an operator might accept. Each row says what it is: the
// flow, and the standing of the rise that produced it. The 15 K and 20 K rows are where a card blown through
// a printed scroll usually lands; the 5 K row is what a quiet build would want; none of them is a claim
// about where the A16's junction will actually sit, which needs the installed measurement exhaust_thermal
// already asks for.
fn airflow_required_at_rises() -> List<String> {
  let watts = match nvidia_a16_board_facts.board_power {
    Present { value: bp } => watt_count(w: gpu_board_power_ceiling(spec: bp)) as Int
    Absent => 0
  }
  let rises = [5, 10, 15, 20, 30]
  map(rises, k => match required_flow(heat: watt(count: watts), rise: celsius_delta(count: k)) {
    Absent => join(["at ", to_string(value: k), " K of rise across the whole air path: no such flow can be named -- the inputs asked for something the arithmetic cannot carry"], "")
    Present { value: flow } => join(["at ", to_string(value: k), " K of rise across the whole air path, one A16 at its maximum ",
                                     to_string(value: watts), " W needs at least ", to_string(value: millilitre_per_second_count(f: flow)),
                                     " mL/s of free air (", free_air_cfm_text(flow: flow),
                                     ") -- the rise is an operator allowance, not a reading"], "")
  })
}

// WHAT THE BFB1012M ACTUALLY MOVES, for comparison, and the standing of that figure: a transcribed free-air
// rating from the listing, at zero static pressure, which is not the flow it will deliver in a scroll.
fn bfb1012m_flow_context() -> String {
  let flow = delta_bfb1012m_rating.free_air_flow
  join(["the BFB1012M is rated at ", to_string(value: millilitre_per_second_count(f: flow)), " mL/s free air -- ", free_air_cfm_text(flow: flow),
        ", at zero static pressure and with the listing's standing (TranscribedUncited, not an instrument reading). Against the curve above it says whether a scroll of the size the chassis accepts could move the heat at all"], "")
}

// The report at a stated wheel-width ratio, and the ratio this repository reports at by default. The ratio
// is NOT from a datasheet -- there is none -- it is a named placeholder for a hypothetical shape, chosen in

